WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier
Reviewer: Camille Martínez

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:16.415
So I'm starting us out today
with a historical mystery.

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:20.006
In 1957, there were two young women,

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.385
both in their 20s,

00:00:21.409 --> 00:00:22.893
both living in the same city,

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.753
both members of the same political group.

00:00:26.751 --> 00:00:30.591
That year, both decided
to commit violent attacks.

00:00:31.284 --> 00:00:35.413
One girl took a gun and approached
a soldier at a checkpoint.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.078
The other girl took a bomb
and went to a crowded café.

00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:43.986
But here's the thing:

00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:48.138
one of the those girls
followed through with the attack,

00:00:49.028 --> 00:00:52.048
but the other turned back.

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:54.997
So what made the difference?

00:00:56.157 --> 00:00:59.414
I'm a behavioral historian,
and I study aggression,

00:00:59.438 --> 00:01:01.359
moral cognition

00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:04.592
and decision-making in social movements.

00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:06.613
That's a mouthful. (Laughs)

00:01:06.637 --> 00:01:09.287
So, the translation of that is:

00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:14.089
I study the moment an individual
decides to pull the trigger,

00:01:14.113 --> 00:01:18.749
the day-to-day decisions
that led up to that moment

00:01:18.773 --> 00:01:24.896
and the stories that they tell themselves
about why that behavior is justified.

00:01:25.468 --> 00:01:26.724
Now, this topic --

00:01:26.748 --> 00:01:29.418
it's not just scholarly for me.

00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:31.227
It's actually a bit personal.

00:01:31.251 --> 00:01:34.612
I grew up in Kootenai County, Idaho,

00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:36.724
and this is very important.

00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:41.669
This is not the part of Idaho
with potatoes.

00:01:41.693 --> 00:01:44.111
We have no potatoes.

00:01:44.135 --> 00:01:46.108
And if you ask me about potatoes,

00:01:46.132 --> 00:01:47.483
I will find you.

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:48.726
(Laughter)

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.198
This part of Idaho is known
for mountain lakes,

00:01:52.222 --> 00:01:54.047
horseback riding,

00:01:54.071 --> 00:01:55.319
skiing.

00:01:55.684 --> 00:01:58.938
Unfortunately, starting in the 1980s,

00:01:58.962 --> 00:02:02.969
it also became known
as the worldwide headquarters

00:02:02.993 --> 00:02:04.984
for the Aryan Nations.

00:02:05.348 --> 00:02:08.911
Every year, members of the local
neo-Nazi compound

00:02:08.935 --> 00:02:11.924
would turn out and march through our town,

00:02:11.948 --> 00:02:13.158
and every year,

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:16.523
members of our town
would turn out and protest them.

00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:20.395
Now, in 2001, I graduated
from high school,

00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:24.577
and I went to college in New York City.

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:28.553
I arrived in August 2001.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.766
As many of you probably are aware,

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.681
three weeks later,

00:02:33.705 --> 00:02:35.500
the Twin Towers went down.

00:02:36.243 --> 00:02:39.997
Now, I was shocked.

00:02:40.503 --> 00:02:42.878
I was incredibly angry.

00:02:44.613 --> 00:02:46.222
I wanted to do something,

00:02:46.246 --> 00:02:50.396
but the only thing that I could think
of doing at that time

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.623
was to study Arabic.

00:02:53.955 --> 00:02:55.112
I will admit,

00:02:55.136 --> 00:03:01.223
I was that girl in class
that wanted to know why "they" hate "us."

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:04.638
I started studying Arabic
for very wrong reasons.

00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:07.200
But something unexpected happened.

00:03:07.224 --> 00:03:10.194
I got a scholarship to go study in Israel.

00:03:10.718 --> 00:03:13.535
So the Idaho girl went to the Middle East.

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:18.045
And while I was there,
I met Palestinian Muslims,

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:20.101
Palestinian Christians,

00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:21.282
Israeli settlers,

00:03:21.306 --> 00:03:22.975
Israeli peace activists.

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:28.035
And what I learned
is that every act has an ecology.

00:03:28.696 --> 00:03:30.125
It has a context.

00:03:32.074 --> 00:03:35.627
Now, since then, I have gone
around the world,

00:03:35.651 --> 00:03:39.565
I have studied violent movements,

00:03:39.589 --> 00:03:44.554
I have worked with NGOs
and ex-combatants in Iraq,

00:03:44.578 --> 00:03:45.776
Syria,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.219
Vietnam,

00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:48.425
the Balkans,

00:03:48.449 --> 00:03:49.730
Cuba.

00:03:50.422 --> 00:03:52.817
I earned my PhD in History,

00:03:52.841 --> 00:03:55.355
and now what I do is
I go to different archives

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:57.600
and I dig through documents,

00:03:57.624 --> 00:04:01.257
looking for police confessions,

00:04:01.281 --> 00:04:03.846
court cases,

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:09.086
diaries and manifestos of individuals
involved in violent attacks.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.296
Now, you gather all these documents --

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:13.730
what do they tell you?

00:04:13.754 --> 00:04:16.921
Our brains love causal mysteries,

00:04:16.945 --> 00:04:18.133
it turns out.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:21.107
So any time we see an attack on the news,

00:04:21.131 --> 00:04:23.563
we tend to ask one question:

00:04:23.587 --> 00:04:24.889
Why?

00:04:24.913 --> 00:04:26.404
Why did that happen?

00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:29.328
Well, I can tell you I've read
thousands of manifestos,

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:33.913
and what you find out is
that they are actually imitative.

00:04:33.937 --> 00:04:37.506
They imitate the political movement
that they're drawing from.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.543
So they actually don't tell us
a lot about decision-making

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:43.561
in that particular case.

00:04:43.924 --> 00:04:48.542
So we have to teach ourselves
to ask a totally different question.

00:04:48.566 --> 00:04:52.144
Instead of "Why?" we have to ask "How?"

00:04:52.168 --> 00:04:55.079
How did individuals produce these attacks,

00:04:55.103 --> 00:05:00.381
and how did their decision-making ecology
contribute to violent behavior?

00:05:00.781 --> 00:05:05.799
There's a couple things I've learned
from asking this kind of question.

00:05:05.823 --> 00:05:07.895
The most important thing is that

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:10.955
political violence is not
culturally endemic.

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:12.359
We create it.

00:05:12.383 --> 00:05:14.673
And whether we realize it or not,

00:05:14.697 --> 00:05:20.160
our day-to-day habits contribute
to the creation of violence

00:05:20.184 --> 00:05:21.738
in our environment.

00:05:21.762 --> 00:05:26.978
So here's a couple of habits
that I've learned contribute to violence.

00:05:28.322 --> 00:05:31.562
One of the first things that attackers did

00:05:31.586 --> 00:05:35.392
when preparing themselves
for a violent event

00:05:35.416 --> 00:05:39.111
was they enclosed themselves
in an information bubble.

00:05:39.135 --> 00:05:41.693
We've heard of fake news, yeah?

00:05:41.717 --> 00:05:44.094
Well, this shocked me:

00:05:44.118 --> 00:05:47.616
every group that I studied
had some kind of a fake news slogan.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.028
French communists
called it the "putrid press."

00:05:51.052 --> 00:05:55.149
French ultranationalists called it
the "sellout press"

00:05:55.173 --> 00:05:57.365
and the "treasonous press."

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:00.900
Islamists in Egypt called it
the "depraved news."

00:06:00.924 --> 00:06:03.989
And Egyptian communists called it ...

00:06:04.013 --> 00:06:05.266
"fake news."

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:09.796
So why do groups spend all this time
trying to make these information bubbles?

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.701
The answer is actually really simple.

00:06:12.725 --> 00:06:17.220
We make decisions based on
the information we trust, yeah?

00:06:17.244 --> 00:06:21.087
So if we trust bad information,

00:06:21.111 --> 00:06:24.086
we're going to make bad decisions.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.100
Another interesting habit
that individuals used

00:06:27.124 --> 00:06:30.067
when they wanted
to produce a violent attack

00:06:30.091 --> 00:06:33.621
was that they looked at their victim
not as an individual

00:06:33.645 --> 00:06:36.356
but just as a member of an opposing team.

00:06:37.006 --> 00:06:38.737
Now this gets really weird.

00:06:39.554 --> 00:06:43.889
There's some fun brain science behind
why that kind of thinking is effective.

00:06:43.913 --> 00:06:47.441
Say I divide all of you guys
into two teams:

00:06:47.465 --> 00:06:48.617
blue team,

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:49.945
red team.

00:06:49.969 --> 00:06:53.437
And then I ask you to compete
in a game against each other.

00:06:53.461 --> 00:06:57.419
Well, the funny thing is,
within milliseconds,

00:06:57.443 --> 00:07:02.292
you will actually start experiencing
pleasure -- pleasure --

00:07:02.316 --> 00:07:07.129
when something bad happens
to members of the other team.

00:07:07.814 --> 00:07:11.898
The funny thing about that is
if I ask one of you blue team members

00:07:11.922 --> 00:07:13.837
to go and join the red team,

00:07:14.805 --> 00:07:16.797
your brain recalibrates,

00:07:16.821 --> 00:07:18.195
and within milliseconds,

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:20.812
you will now start experiencing pleasure

00:07:20.836 --> 00:07:24.294
when bad things happen
to members of your old team.

00:07:26.064 --> 00:07:32.669
This is a really good example
of why us-them thinking is so dangerous

00:07:32.693 --> 00:07:34.463
in our political environment.

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:38.828
Another habit that attackers used
to kind of rev themselves up for an attack

00:07:38.852 --> 00:07:41.220
was they focused on differences.

00:07:41.244 --> 00:07:44.201
In other words, they looked
at their victims, and they thought,

00:07:45.075 --> 00:07:47.221
"I share nothing in common
with that person.

00:07:47.245 --> 00:07:49.374
They are totally different than me."

00:07:50.829 --> 00:07:53.995
Again, this might sound
like a really simple concept,

00:07:54.019 --> 00:07:58.676
but there's some fascinating science
behind why this works.

00:07:59.209 --> 00:08:04.438
Say I show you guys videos
of different-colored hands

00:08:04.462 --> 00:08:08.237
and sharp pins being driven
into these different-colored hands,

00:08:08.261 --> 00:08:09.411
OK?

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.219
If you're white,

00:08:12.243 --> 00:08:17.954
the chances are you will experience
the most sympathetic activation,

00:08:17.978 --> 00:08:19.541
or the most pain,

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:22.496
when you see a pin
going into the white hand.

00:08:24.053 --> 00:08:27.447
If you are Latin American, Arab, Black,

00:08:27.471 --> 00:08:31.038
you will probably experience
the most sympathetic activation

00:08:31.062 --> 00:08:35.926
watching a pin going into the hand
that looks most like yours.

00:08:38.846 --> 00:08:42.718
The good news is,
that's not biologically fixed.

00:08:42.742 --> 00:08:44.537
That is learned behavior.

00:08:45.252 --> 00:08:49.730
Which means the more we spend time
with other ethnic communities

00:08:49.754 --> 00:08:56.623
and the more we see them as similar to us
and part of our team,

00:08:56.647 --> 00:08:58.875
the more we feel their pain.

00:08:58.899 --> 00:09:01.456
The last habit
that I'm going to talk about

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:06.537
is when attackers prepared themselves
to go out and do one of these events,

00:09:06.561 --> 00:09:09.227
they focused on certain emotional cues.

00:09:09.251 --> 00:09:15.117
For months, they geared themselves up
by focusing on anger cues, for instance.

00:09:15.141 --> 00:09:17.839
I bring this up because
it's really popular right now.

00:09:17.863 --> 00:09:21.547
If you read blogs or the news,

00:09:21.571 --> 00:09:25.550
you see talk of two concepts
from laboratory science:

00:09:25.574 --> 00:09:28.779
amygdala hijacking
and emotional hijacking.

00:09:28.803 --> 00:09:31.376
Now, amygdala hijacking:

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:35.489
it's the concept that I show you
a cue -- say, a gun --

00:09:35.513 --> 00:09:39.406
and your brain reacts
with an automatic threat response

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:40.599
to that cue.

00:09:40.623 --> 00:09:43.124
Emotional hijacking --
it's a very similar concept.

00:09:43.148 --> 00:09:48.179
It's the idea that I show you
an anger cue, for instance,

00:09:48.203 --> 00:09:53.292
and your brain will react
with an automatic anger response

00:09:53.316 --> 00:09:54.643
to that cue.

00:09:54.667 --> 00:09:58.646
I think women usually get
this more than men. (Laughs)

00:09:58.670 --> 00:09:59.677
(Laughter)

00:09:59.701 --> 00:10:03.227
That kind of a hijacking narrative
grabs our attention.

00:10:03.251 --> 00:10:06.016
Just the word "hijacking"
grabs our attention.

00:10:06.526 --> 00:10:07.677
The thing is,

00:10:07.701 --> 00:10:12.472
most of the time, that's not really
how cues work in real life.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.120
If you study history,

00:10:14.144 --> 00:10:19.406
what you find is that we are bombarded
with hundreds of thousands of cues

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:20.837
every day.

00:10:20.861 --> 00:10:22.908
And so what we do is we learn to filter.

00:10:22.932 --> 00:10:24.776
We ignore some cues,

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.979
we pay attention to other cues.

00:10:27.003 --> 00:10:30.631
For political violence,
this becomes really important,

00:10:30.655 --> 00:10:36.566
because what it meant is that attackers
usually didn't just see an anger cue

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.470
and suddenly snap.

00:10:38.825 --> 00:10:40.288
Instead,

00:10:40.312 --> 00:10:46.831
politicians, social activists
spent weeks, months, years

00:10:46.855 --> 00:10:51.878
flooding the environment
with anger cues, for instance,

00:10:51.902 --> 00:10:53.905
and attackers,

00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:56.364
they paid attention to those cues,

00:10:56.388 --> 00:10:58.914
they trusted those cues,

00:10:58.938 --> 00:11:00.486
they focused on them,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:03.112
they even memorized those cues.

00:11:03.136 --> 00:11:09.613
All of this just really goes to show
how important it is to study history.

00:11:09.637 --> 00:11:13.607
It's one thing to see how cues operate
in a laboratory setting.

00:11:13.631 --> 00:11:17.245
And those laboratory experiments
are incredibly important.

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:21.491
They give us a lot of new data
about how our bodies work.

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:27.488
But it's also very important to see
how those cues operate in real life.

00:11:30.535 --> 00:11:34.700
So what does all this tell us
about political violence?

00:11:35.908 --> 00:11:39.496
Political violence is not
culturally endemic.

00:11:39.985 --> 00:11:45.208
It is not an automatic, predetermined
response to environmental stimuli.

00:11:45.523 --> 00:11:46.706
We produce it.

00:11:47.356 --> 00:11:49.429
Our everyday habits produce it.

00:11:50.945 --> 00:11:54.906
Let's go back, actually, to those two
women that I mentioned at the start.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:12:01.518
The first woman had been paying attention
to those outrage campaigns,

00:12:01.542 --> 00:12:02.965
so she took a gun

00:12:02.989 --> 00:12:05.258
and approached a soldier at a checkpoint.

00:12:07.302 --> 00:12:10.905
But in that moment,
something really interesting happened.

00:12:10.929 --> 00:12:13.798
She looked at that soldier,

00:12:13.822 --> 00:12:15.775
and she thought to herself,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.762
"He's the same age as me.

00:12:21.435 --> 00:12:22.953
He looks like me."

00:12:24.724 --> 00:12:27.262
And she put down the gun,
and she walked away.

00:12:28.179 --> 00:12:30.602
Just from that little bit of similarity.

00:12:32.128 --> 00:12:35.702
The second girl had
a totally different outcome.

00:12:37.533 --> 00:12:40.382
She also listened
to the outrage campaigns,

00:12:40.406 --> 00:12:43.398
but she surrounded herself
with individuals

00:12:43.422 --> 00:12:45.246
who were supportive of violence,

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.776
with peers who supported her violence.

00:12:48.707 --> 00:12:51.983
She enclosed herself
in an information bubble.

00:12:52.747 --> 00:12:56.397
She focused on certain
emotional cues for months.

00:12:56.421 --> 00:13:02.013
She taught herself to bypass certain
cultural inhibitions against violence.

00:13:02.037 --> 00:13:03.784
She practiced her plan,

00:13:03.808 --> 00:13:06.055
she taught herself new habits,

00:13:06.079 --> 00:13:10.201
and when the time came,
she took her bomb to the café,

00:13:10.225 --> 00:13:12.526
and she followed through with that attack.

00:13:15.592 --> 00:13:18.403
This was not impulse.

00:13:18.903 --> 00:13:20.576
This was learning.

00:13:22.463 --> 00:13:26.345
Polarization in our society
is not impulse,

00:13:26.369 --> 00:13:27.715
it's learning.

00:13:28.391 --> 00:13:31.318
Every day we are teaching ourselves:

00:13:31.342 --> 00:13:33.304
the news we click on,

00:13:33.328 --> 00:13:35.372
the emotions that we focus on,

00:13:35.396 --> 00:13:39.756
the thoughts that we entertain
about the red team or the blue team.

00:13:40.284 --> 00:13:42.549
All of this contributes to learning,

00:13:42.573 --> 00:13:44.296
whether we realize it or not.

00:13:44.696 --> 00:13:46.324
The good news

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:53.441
is that while the individuals I study
already made their decisions,

00:13:53.465 --> 00:13:55.885
we can still change our trajectory.

00:13:57.164 --> 00:14:00.824
We might never make
the decisions that they made,

00:14:00.848 --> 00:14:04.997
but we can stop contributing
to violent ecologies.

00:14:05.552 --> 00:14:10.038
We can get out of whatever
news bubble we're in,

00:14:10.062 --> 00:14:14.106
we can be more mindful
about the emotional cues

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:15.359
that we focus on,

00:14:15.383 --> 00:14:17.782
the outrage bait that we click on.

00:14:18.422 --> 00:14:19.602
But most importantly,

00:14:19.626 --> 00:14:24.127
we can stop seeing each other
as just members of the red team

00:14:24.151 --> 00:14:25.545
or the blue team.

00:14:25.959 --> 00:14:31.856
Because whether we are Christian,
Muslim, Jewish, atheist,

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.364
Democrat or Republican,

00:14:34.388 --> 00:14:35.544
we're human.

00:14:35.568 --> 00:14:36.843
We're human beings.

00:14:37.992 --> 00:14:41.494
And we often share really similar habits.

00:14:42.224 --> 00:14:44.057
We have differences.

00:14:44.081 --> 00:14:46.296
Those differences are beautiful,

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.761
and those differences are very important.

00:14:48.785 --> 00:14:55.254
But our future depends on us
being able to find common ground

00:14:55.278 --> 00:14:56.628
with the other side.

00:14:57.785 --> 00:15:01.120
And that's why it is so, so important

00:15:01.144 --> 00:15:03.723
for us to retrain our brains

00:15:03.747 --> 00:15:07.547
and stop contributing
to violent ecologies.

00:15:08.272 --> 00:15:09.444
Thank you.

00:15:09.468 --> 00:15:11.374
(Applause)

